        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "refactor(qa): canonicalize channel command scenarios",
        "body": "Closes #101015\n\n## What Problem This Solves\n\nChannel command and session-context QA was split between imperative Telegram and WhatsApp runners and the canonical scenario catalog. That duplication made the shared QA host and bridge adapters unable to exercise the same definitions for help, command catalogs, tool listings, identity, status, context, new sessions, authorization, current-session tools, usage footers, and exact-marker replies.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nMoves the command and session-context checks into canonical YAML flows, delegates the Telegram and WhatsApp CLI aliases to the shared runtime launcher, and removes the replaced imperative owners. Remaining transport-specific scenarios continue through their existing runners, with mixed selections validated before any live traffic starts.\n\n## User Impact\n\nQA operators can run the same command and session-context definitions through the shared QA host, Telegram Crabline bridge, or applicable live adapters. Existing Telegram and WhatsApp QA commands keep their aliases and remaining default coverage.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Blacksmith Testbox `tbx_01kww4aezwg5t21ywg2nzrrfqr` (`amber-prawn`)\n- Exact-head `pnpm build`: passed\n- Focused QA Lab tests: 7 files, 261 tests passed\n- QA Channel: 7 command/exact-marker scenarios passed\n- Crabline Telegram: exact-marker scenario passed\n- Path-scoped changed checks passed through runtime guards; remote typecheck was blocked because Crabbox full sync omitted the tracked `extensions/qa-lab/src/mantis` subtree from current `main`\n- Fresh bounded autoreview: no accepted/actionable findings\n",
